<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Posts on Tech and Lifestyle Blog</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/</link><description>Recent content in Posts on Tech and Lifestyle Blog</description><generator>Hugo -- gohugo.io</generator><language>en</language><copyright>© 2025 Yalchin Mammadli</copyright><lastBuildDate>Thu, 28 Aug 2025 21:08:48 +0200</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Step by Step Guide to Autogenerate Python Documentation With Sphinx</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/step-by-step-guide-to-autogenerate-python-documentation-with-sphinx/</link><pubDate>Thu, 28 Aug 2025 21:08:48 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/step-by-step-guide-to-autogenerate-python-documentation-with-sphinx/</guid><description>&lt;h1 class="relative group"&gt;Creating Autogenerated Sphinx Documentation for Python Projects
&lt;div id="creating-autogenerated-sphinx-documentation-for-python-projects" class="anchor"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100 select-none"&gt;
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700 !no-underline" href="#creating-autogenerated-sphinx-documentation-for-python-projects" aria-label="Anchor"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Sphinx is a powerful documentation generator that makes it easy to create intelligent and beautiful documentation for Python projects. In this guide, we&amp;rsquo;ll walk through the process of setting up Sphinx and using it to autogenerate documentation from your Python source code.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 class="relative group"&gt;Setting Up the Environment
&lt;div id="setting-up-the-environment" class="anchor"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100 select-none"&gt;
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700 !no-underline" href="#setting-up-the-environment" aria-label="Anchor"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;First, let&amp;rsquo;s create a virtual environment and install the necessary packages:&lt;/p&gt;</description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/step-by-step-guide-to-autogenerate-python-documentation-with-sphinx/featured.png"/></item><item><title>Basic Vim Cheat Sheet</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/basic-vim-cheat-sheet/</link><pubDate>Fri, 22 Aug 2025 22:46:44 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/basic-vim-cheat-sheet/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Vim is a highly configurable text editor built to make creating and changing any kind of text very efficient. It is included as &amp;ldquo;vi&amp;rdquo; with most UNIX systems and with Apple OS X. This written tutorial will help you to understand basic key bindings of Vim. You can also refer to this tutorial in order to use it as your cheat sheet.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 class="relative group"&gt;Your Cheat Sheet
&lt;div id="your-cheat-sheet" class="anchor"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100 select-none"&gt;
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700 !no-underline" href="#your-cheat-sheet" aria-label="Anchor"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;No&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Command&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Meaning&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;:w&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Save&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;:q&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Quit&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;:q!&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Quit without saving&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;4&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;:wq&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Quit with saving&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;5&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;k&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Navigates up&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;6&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;j&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Navigates down&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;7&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;h&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Navigates left&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;8&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;l&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Navigates right&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;9&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;i&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Enter insert mode&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;10&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;esc or ctrl +[&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Enter normal mode&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;11&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;gg&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Navigates to the top&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;12&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;G&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Navigates to bottom&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;13&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;H&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;High: takes you to the top of your code&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;14&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;M&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Middle: takes you to the middle of your code&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;15&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;L&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Low: takes you to the bottom of your code&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;16&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;nG&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Navigates to n-th line (example - 3G navigates to 3rd line)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;17&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;{&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Navigates one code block up&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;18&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;}&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Navigates one code block down&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;19&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;nk&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;N lines up&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;20&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;nj&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;N lines down&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;21&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;nh&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;N lines left&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;22&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;nl&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;n lines right&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;23&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;u&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Undo&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;24&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;ctrl + r&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Redo&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;25&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;. (period)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Do it again&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;26&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;yy&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Copy&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;27&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;P&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Paste above&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;28&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;p&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Paste below&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;29&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;v&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Switches to visual mode&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;30&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;o&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Adds new line below and enters insert mode&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;31&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;O&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Adds new line up and puts you inside insert mode&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;32&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;w&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Navigate to next word&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;33&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;b&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Navigate to previous word&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;34&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;:n&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Takes you to the n-th line&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;35&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0 (zero)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Navigates to the beginning of line&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;36&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;^ or 0w&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Navigates to the beginning of the first word in the line&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;37&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;W&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Navigates to next word (ignores punctuation)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;38&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;B&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Navigates to previous word (ignores punctuation)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;39&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;t&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Navigates to the specified character and put the cursor one before the specified character (example: t!)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;40&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;f&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;to the specified character and put the cursor on the specified character (example: f!)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;41&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Navigates to the next same character&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;42&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;cw&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Changes next word&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;43&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;dw&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Deletes next word&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;44&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;D&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Deletes rest of the line (after coursor)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;45&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;C&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Deletes rest of the line (after coursor) and enters insert mode&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;46&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;ct&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Then press the letter that you want to change up to, will delete till that letter and enter insert mode&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;47&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;dt&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Then press the letter that you want to change up to, will delete till that letter&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;48&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;*&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Searches for current word&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;49&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Goes to next instance&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;50&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;a&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Takes you to the next character and put you into insert mode&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;51&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Navigates to the end of the line and enters insert mode&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;52&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;I&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Takes you to at the beginning of the line and puts you into insert mode&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;53&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Swaps the letter (upper to lower case or vice versa)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;54&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;/&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Searches particular word or character, press &amp;ldquo;Enter&amp;rdquo; to find. (Then you can use n to navigate next occurrence or N to the previous)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;55&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;:num&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Navigates to specified line. Example: :55 will take you 55th line&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;</description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/basic-vim-cheat-sheet/featured.png"/></item><item><title>What Is Virtual Environment in Python and How to Set Up Virtual Environment</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/what-is-virtual-environment-in-python-and-how-to-set-up-virtual-environment/</link><pubDate>Fri, 22 Aug 2025 22:44:47 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/what-is-virtual-environment-in-python-and-how-to-set-up-virtual-environment/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;A bit of theory first&amp;hellip;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If you are using Python, you will always need to use a virtual environment if you want to keep the packages you installed as organized and neat as possible. So basically virtual environment will make it possible to have different versions of the same library or framework for each project. Let&amp;rsquo;s say you are a great Django developer (I believe you are :D), and for god knows whatever reason you want to have &lt;em&gt;Django version 3.0.0&lt;/em&gt; for one project and &lt;em&gt;version 3.2&lt;/em&gt; for another. So in this case, what you can do is you can create a separate virtual environment for each project and use different versions for packages. Another advantage of using a virtual environment for me is to have the ability to create a &lt;em&gt;requirements.txt&lt;/em&gt; file that can be used in the project setup. Well, you can tell me that you can create it without a virtual environment but if you do so, &lt;em&gt;requirements.txt&lt;/em&gt; file will contain all of the packages that have been installed globally on your PC. You can see the content by executing:&lt;/p&gt;</description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/what-is-virtual-environment-in-python-and-how-to-set-up-virtual-environment/featured.png"/></item><item><title>How to Secure Ipfs Node</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-secure-ipfs-node/</link><pubDate>Tue, 19 Aug 2025 13:36:21 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-secure-ipfs-node/</guid><description>&lt;h1 class="relative group"&gt;Why You Should Never Expose the IPFS RPC API Publicly (and How to Secure It)
&lt;div id="why-you-should-never-expose-the-ipfs-rpc-api-publicly-and-how-to-secure-it" class="anchor"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100 select-none"&gt;
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700 !no-underline" href="#why-you-should-never-expose-the-ipfs-rpc-api-publicly-and-how-to-secure-it" aria-label="Anchor"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When running an &lt;a
href="https://ipfs.tech/"
target="_blank"
&gt;IPFS&lt;/a&gt; node, one of the most important security considerations is how you handle &lt;strong&gt;ports&lt;/strong&gt; and the &lt;strong&gt;RPC API&lt;/strong&gt;. Many new users unknowingly expose their node’s RPC API to the internet, leaving it wide open for abuse. In this post, we’ll explore why this is dangerous and how you can protect yourself with IPFS’s built-in &lt;strong&gt;basic authentication&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-secure-ipfs-node/featured.png"/></item><item><title>Sorting Algorithms and Binary Search in Python</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/sorting-algorithms-and-binary-search-in-python/</link><pubDate>Fri, 16 May 2025 21:43:57 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/sorting-algorithms-and-binary-search-in-python/</guid><description>&lt;h2 class="relative group"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Selection Sort&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;div id="selection-sort" class="anchor"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100 select-none"&gt;
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700 !no-underline" href="#selection-sort" aria-label="Anchor"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Selection sort is about selecting the minimum value in an unsorted array and moving it towards the front by comparing. Here is an example code snippet to implement binary search in Python:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-py" data-lang="py"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;selection_sort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;list_&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;len&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;list_&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;index&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;range&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;list_&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;index&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;j&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;range&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;index&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;list_&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;j&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;list_&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;j&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;list_&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;j&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;list_&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;index&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;list_&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;index&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;list_&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;j&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;list_&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;unsorted_list&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;7&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;9&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;7&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;6&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;6&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;7&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;9&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;7&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;6&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;6&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;7&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;selection_sort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;unsorted_list&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;h3 class="relative group"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Buble Sort&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;div id="buble-sort" class="anchor"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100 select-none"&gt;
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700 !no-underline" href="#buble-sort" aria-label="Anchor"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The bubble sort algorithm is applied by going through an array of data a number of times and at the same time comparing two adjacent numbers at a time in order to reorder them if there are out of order. Here is an example snippet written in Python:&lt;/p&gt;</description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/sorting-algorithms-and-binary-search-in-python/featured.png"/></item><item><title>Map Sort Filter Functions in Python</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/map-sort-filter-functions-in-python/</link><pubDate>Fri, 16 May 2025 21:28:34 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/map-sort-filter-functions-in-python/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;This tutorial will help you to see real example of using map, sort, and filter functions along with lambda functions in Python.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Map Function Example&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Python&amp;rsquo;s map() is a built-in function that allows you to process and transform all the items in an iterable without using an explicit for loop, a technique commonly known as mapping. map() is useful when you need to apply a transformation function to each item in an iterable and transform them into a new iterable.&lt;/p&gt;</description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/map-sort-filter-functions-in-python/featured.png"/></item><item><title>Implementing Database Logs or History</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/implementing-database-logs-or-history/</link><pubDate>Fri, 16 May 2025 21:22:57 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/implementing-database-logs-or-history/</guid><description>&lt;h2 class="relative group"&gt;Introduction
&lt;div id="introduction" class="anchor"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100 select-none"&gt;
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700 !no-underline" href="#introduction" aria-label="Anchor"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In the realm of database management, maintaining an accurate record of changes made to the database structure is paramount. This is where the concept of a database changelog comes into play. A changelog, also known as a record history or audit trail, serves as a chronological log of modifications made to the database schema or data. It plays a crucial role in ensuring data integrity, transparency, and accountability within an organization&amp;rsquo;s data management processes.&lt;/p&gt;</description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/implementing-database-logs-or-history/featured.png"/></item><item><title>Python Decorators With Function and Decorator Parameters</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/python-decorators-with-function-and-decorator-parameters/</link><pubDate>Fri, 16 May 2025 21:17:13 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/python-decorators-with-function-and-decorator-parameters/</guid><description>&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-py" data-lang="py"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;modify_multiply_decorator&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;args&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;**&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;kwargs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# coeff=3&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;dec&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;multiply_func&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;inner&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;nums&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# (2, 290)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;coefficient&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kwargs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;&amp;#34;coefficient&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# 3&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;multiply_func&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;nums&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# 2 * 290&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;modified_result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;coefficient&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# 3 * 580&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;modified_result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# 1740&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;inner&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dec&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-py" data-lang="py"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@modify_multiply_decorator&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;coefficient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;multiply&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;nums&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;nums&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;nums&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;multiply&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;290&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/python-decorators-with-function-and-decorator-parameters/featured.png"/></item><item><title>How to Setup Mailgun in Django</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-setup-mailgun-in-django/</link><pubDate>Thu, 15 May 2025 12:29:36 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-setup-mailgun-in-django/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;In this tutorial, I will try to cover configuring a custom domain for sending emails in your application. The benefit of using mailgun is that it allows you to send (5,000) emails for free during the first three months (then you will pay $0.8 per 1,000 emails) and its setup procedure is so straightforward. You will only need to add your payment verification to be able to send emails without any restrictions.&lt;/p&gt;</description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-setup-mailgun-in-django/featured.png"/></item><item><title>How to Setup Github Ssh Key</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-setup-github-ssh-key/</link><pubDate>Thu, 15 May 2025 12:27:47 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-setup-github-ssh-key/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Github SSH Key set up&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This tutorial will help you to be able to set up SSH key connection to your github account on a &lt;strong&gt;Linux&lt;/strong&gt; machine. You can also continue to read this tutorial if you want to set up &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Deploy Keys&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For now let&amp;rsquo;s just assume that &lt;strong&gt;you&amp;rsquo;ve successfully set up SSH key&lt;/strong&gt;, after that you need to make sure that you don&amp;rsquo;t access your repo with HTTPS, but with ssh, so if you previously cloned your repo wirh https set it to ssh as below:&lt;/p&gt;</description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-setup-github-ssh-key/featured.png"/></item><item><title>How to Deploy Nodejs App</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-deploy-nodejs-app/</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 May 2025 00:53:10 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-deploy-nodejs-app/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;In this tutorial, we will be deploying a simple URL shortener API built-in express framework of NodeJs. It is pretty simple, uses MongoDB as its database, and you can find its source code &lt;a
href="https://github.com/Yalchin403/express-urlshortener-api"
target="_blank"
&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As a VPS, I use a Linode ubuntu server, and I assume that you have at least some basic knowledge when it comes to Linux.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3 class="relative group"&gt;Install Node and NPM
&lt;div id="install-node-and-npm" class="anchor"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100 select-none"&gt;
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700 !no-underline" href="#install-node-and-npm" aria-label="Anchor"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-sh" data-lang="sh"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;curl -sL https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_12.x &lt;span class="p"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; sudo -E bash -
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;sudo apt install nodejs
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# verify that bash is installed&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;node --version
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;h3 class="relative group"&gt;Clone your Repo
&lt;div id="clone-your-repo" class="anchor"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100 select-none"&gt;
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700 !no-underline" href="#clone-your-repo" aria-label="Anchor"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;git clone [link to your github repo]&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-deploy-nodejs-app/featured.png"/></item><item><title>Accent and Case Insensitive Search and Minimum Distance Calculator in Pymongo</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/accent-and-case-sensitive-search-pymongo/</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 May 2025 00:51:03 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/accent-and-case-sensitive-search-pymongo/</guid><description>&lt;h1 class="relative group"&gt;Ellaborate case
&lt;div id="ellaborate-case" class="anchor"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100 select-none"&gt;
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700 !no-underline" href="#ellaborate-case" aria-label="Anchor"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Before starting with set up and everything, I would love to confess that I find Mongodb and Python combination as &lt;strong&gt;pain in the ass&lt;/strong&gt;. Recently I have got an job offer as Python/Flask developer in one of the local companies in my country. They are using Mongodb as their production database. They requested a little bit of task to accomplish before we move on to HR interview. So the task was about coding autocomplete search logic and also finding the closest location to our current location from the database. Task seems interesting and pretty much easy to solve, at least for me as a Django developer who have used Postgresql as default database in most of the cases. However, seemed like things don&amp;rsquo;t go so well with Mongodb in django. It seemed like if Python is envolved, we normally have two pypi packages two use, namely &lt;a
href="https://pymongo.readthedocs.io/en/stable/"
target="_blank"
&gt;pymongo&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a
href="https://docs.mongoengine.org/"
target="_blank"
&gt;mongoengine&lt;/a&gt;. I was familiar with pymongo as in my &lt;a
href="https://t.me/mp3converteryoutubebot"
target="_blank"
&gt;telegram bot&lt;/a&gt; I use Mongodb as database and use pymongo to implement database actions. Hence, below I will explain how I implemented case and accent insensitive search and minimum distance finding via pymongo.&lt;/p&gt;</description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/accent-and-case-sensitive-search-pymongo/featured.png"/></item><item><title>Securely Configure Vps</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/securely-configure-vps/</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 May 2025 00:20:23 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/securely-configure-vps/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;For your information, this tutorial is not written by me absolutely, I&amp;rsquo;ve used ssh connection part of &lt;a
href="https://notthebe.ee/Creating-your-own-OpenVPN-server.html"
target="_blank"
&gt;this&lt;/a&gt; tutorial and added some comments to make individual tutorial on &amp;ldquo;how to create your own vpn&amp;rdquo; and also covers the things that we can implement to make our vps the way more secure.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Generating SSH keys&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Using a cleartext password to log in to your server is never a good idea since the password is not encrypted in transit and can be exposed on a hostile network. By creating an SSH key we&amp;rsquo;re going to make it so that you can only log in to the server if you have the key file and the password, and at the same time the password is encrypted. If you&amp;rsquo;re using Linux you probably already know how to open the terminal, if you&amp;rsquo;re on Mac you can find the Terminal app in your Applications folder, and on Windows 10 you&amp;rsquo;ll need to open the PowerShell with administrator&amp;rsquo;s privileges and install SSH using this command:&lt;/p&gt;</description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/securely-configure-vps/featured.png"/></item></channel></rss>